  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nGusty northwest winds continue to spread east today behind the sfc \ncold front and with the upper trough. There have been a couple gusts \nover 58 mph along and west of the Missouri in the clear air, better \nmixing environment, but nothing widespread enough to merit an \nupgrade. As the upper trough digs in more this evening, the 80 to \n90kt jet will help keep the winds gusty overnight. Ensemble 90th \npercentiles keep gusts to 50 mph over the Coteau and the Sisseton \nHills early Friday morning. Current NBM holds those gusts to around \n40 mph, so did not expand the wind headlines farther east for now. \n\nAnother area of low clouds is wrapping around the low in northern ND \nand pushing south into north central SD this afternoon. These clouds \nare expected to expand and stick around most of the night. Some \nadditional sprinkles or light rain is possible in this \nwraparound/deformation zone. Between the persistent winds and the \nclouds, temperatures should not drop below freezing. Blended in hi-\nres model guidance to push temps up a few degrees. Have lows in the \n30s for tonight with Corson county approaching freezing as sfc high \npressure builds in from the west. Given the winds, decided not to go \nwith any frost headlines for tonight. However, Friday night/Saturday \nmorning will need another look for frost/freeze headlines. There are \nstill model differences in timing of a building ridge aloft and exit \nof the sfc high that will influence Friday night's lows. \n\nTemperatures warm above normal starting Saturday with the ridge \naloft. Dry and warm conditions are then expected through the middle \nof next week.",
